   "typ": "Short Answer",
   "stem": "In a certain species of rabbit, coat color follows autosomal recessive inheritance (brown fur, b, is recessive to black fur, B), and an unrelated ear-shape trait follows X-linked recessive inheritance (floppy ears, e, is recessive to upright ears, E). A black-furred, upright- eared male rabbit that is heterozygous for both traits (genotype BbXEXE... note: males are hemizygous for X-linked genes, so his genotype is BbXEY) is crossed with a black-furred, upright-eared female rabbit who is heterozygous for coat color and is a carrier for the ear- shape trait (genotype BbXEXe). (a) Set up and solve for the expected phenotype ratios among the offspring for coat color alone. (b) Set up and solve for the expected phenotype ratios among the offspring for ear shape alone, making sure to consider male and female offspring separately. (c) Explain why these two traits can be analyzed independently of one another using separate Punnett squares. (Long Answer) MITOSIS AND MEIOSIS",
   "options": [],
   "correct": [],
   "explanation": "",
   "sample": "(a) Coat color (Bb x Bb): expected phenotype ratio 3 black : 1 brown (3/4 black, 1/4 brown), from the standard monohybrid cross. (b) Ear shape (XY x XEXe): Daughters receive their father's XE plus either XE or Xe from their mother, so ALL daughters are phenotypically upright-eared (1/2 XEXE : 1/2 XEXe carrier — 100% upright-eared, but half are carriers). Sons receive their father’s Y plus either X= or X¢ from their mother, so sons are 1/2 upright-eared (XEY) : 1/2 floppy-eared (XeY). (c) The two traits can be analyzed with separate Punnett squares because they assort independently of one another — the coat-color gene is autosomal while the ear-shape gene is X-linked, so (per Mendel's Law of Independent Assortment, extended to genes on different/non- homologous chromosomes) the alleles for one gene are inherited independently of the alleles for the other, meaning the outcome of one cross does not influence the outcome of the other. MITOSIS AND MEIOSIS",
