   "topic": "",
   "typ": "Short Answer",
   "stem": "Describe, in order, the major events of meiosis | and meiosis II, and explain how the overall process differs from mitosis in terms of number of divisions, number and ploidy of resulting cells, and genetic makeup of the resulting cells. Then explain how nondisjunction can occur during EITHER meiosis | or meiosis II, and how the resulting chromosomal abnormality differs depending on which division is affected. (Long Answer) MOLECULAR BIOLOGY OF DNA",
   "options": [],
   "correct": [],
   "explanation": "",
   "sample": "Accept responses that describe, in order: Meiosis | — homologous chromosomes pair up (synapsis) and undergo crossing over during prophase |; homologous pairs align at the metaphase plate during metaphase |; homologous chromosomes (each still consisting of two sister chromatids) separate to opposite poles during anaphase |; two haploid cells form during telophase I/cytokinesis. Meiosis II — closely resembles mitosis: sister chromatids separate during anaphase II, ultimately producing four haploid cells total, each genetically unique due to crossing over and independent assortment in meiosis |. This differs from mitosis in number of divisions (two vs. one), ploidy/number of resulting cells (four haploid cells vs. two diploid cells), and genetic makeup (genetically varied vs. genetically identical). Nondisjunction during meiosis | (failure of homologous chromosomes to separate) affects ALL four resulting gametes — two gametes end up with an extra chromosome, and two end up missing that chromosome entirely. Nondisjunction during meiosis II (failure of sister chromatids to separate, after a normal meiosis |) affects only HALF of the resulting gametes — of the four gametes, one ends up with an extra chromosome, one is missing that chromosome, and the other two (from the meiosis | division that proceeded normally) are chromosomally normal. MOLECULAR BIOLOGY OF DNA",
