   "topic": "",
   "typ": "Short Answer",
   "stem": "In a certain species of dog, coat color follows autosomal recessive inheritance (black coat, B, is dominant to brown coat, b), and an unrelated hearing trait follows X-linked recessive inheritance (normal hearing, H, is dominant to deafness, h). A black-coated, normal-hearing male dog that is heterozygous for coat color (genotype Bb) and hemizygous normal-hearing (genotype XHY) is crossed with a black-coated, normal-hearing female dog who is heterozygous for coat color (Bb) and is a carrier for deafness (genotype XHXh). (a) Set up and solve for the expected phenotype ratio among the offspring for coat color alone. (b) Set up and solve for the expected phenotype ratio among the offspring for hearing status alone, considering male and female offspring separately. (c) Explain why it is valid to calculate the combined probability of a puppy being BOTH brown-coated AND deaf by multiplying the individual probabilities you found in parts (a) and (b). (Long Answer) MITOSIS AND MEIOSIS",
   "options": [],
   "correct": [],
   "explanation": "",
   "sample": "(a) Coat color (Bb x Bb): expected phenotype ratio 3 black : 1 brown. (b) Hearing (XHY x XHXh): Daughters receive their father’s XH plus either XH or X from their mother, so ALL daughters have normal hearing phenotypically (1/2 XHXH : 1/2 XHX) carrier — 100% normal-hearing, half carriers). Sons receive their father's Y plus either XH or Xh from their mother, so sons are 1/2 normal-hearing (XHY) : 1/2 deaf (XY). (c) It is valid to multiply the two probabilities together because the two traits are controlled by different, unrelated genes (one autosomal, one X-linked) that assort independently of one another — since the two events (a puppy's coat-color outcome and its hearing outcome) are independent, the product rule applies, and the combined probability of both specific outcomes occurring together equals the product of their individual probabilities. MITOSIS AND MEIOSIS",
