   "typ": "Short Answer",
   "stem": "A gene's coding sequence experiences three separate mutations in three different individuals: Individual 1 has a single-nucleotide substitution that changes a codon but still codes for the same amino acid. Individual 2 has a single-nucleotide substitution that changes a codon to a premature stop codon. Individual 3 has a single-nucleotide insertion (not a multiple of three) partway through the coding sequence. For EACH individual, name the type of mutation involved, and explain in detail how each mutation is likely to affect the structure and length of the resulting protein compared to the normal, unmutated protein. (Long Answer) GENE & PROTEIN RELATIONSHIP",
   "options": [],
   "correct": [],
   "explanation": "",
   "sample": "Accept responses that correctly name each mutation type and describe its likely effect: Individual 1 — a silent mutation; since the same amino acid is still specified, the resulting protein's amino acid sequence, length, and structure are typically unaffected. Individual 2 — a nonsense mutation; the premature stop codon causes translation to end early, producing a truncated (shorter than normal) protein that is missing part of its normal sequence and is often nonfunctional, especially if the truncation occurs early in the sequence. Individual 3 — a frameshift mutation; because the insertion is not a multiple of three, every codon downstream of the insertion is read incorrectly, typically producing a garbled, largely different amino acid sequence from that point onward and often introducing a premature stop codon somewhere downstream — usually resulting in a protein that differs greatly in both structure and length from the normal protein and is very likely nonfunctional. GENE & PROTEIN RELATIONSHIP",
   "img": null,
   "appears": [
    "National · Div B · Form 2 #51"
   ],
